<http://purl.org/nanopub/temp/np1/assertion> {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A nanopub whose assertion uses Project Delphi v2.0 terms cannot instantiate the template its pipeline declares, because the published claim, evidence-item and rubric-assessment templates still use legacy ProbKnow terms, so every such declaration is a false statement about how the record was made." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "The delphi skill settles that new records use ontology v2.0 terms and never the legacy probknow IRIs. The pipeline's TEMPLATES map nonetheless sends each v2.0 class to the published ProbKnow template for that class, and those templates fix legacy types on legacy subjects. A record that follows the settled rule therefore cannot instantiate the template it declares, and nt:wasCreatedFromTemplate becomes a false statement in its publication info. The defect is in the publication info and describes how a record was made, not what it asserts: assertions built from v2.0 terms are unaffected. It is also not a case of one template version regressing. Migrated templates exist in the ontology source for the claim, evidence-item and rubric-assessment templates; they have not been republished, so the network still serves the legacy copies. The pubinfo-domain and rubric templates are still legacy in the source and would need migrating first. There are two claim templates in play and both are legacy on the network: the one the published records declare, and the one the current template listing shows, labelled \"Project Delphi claim\" and created 2026-10-23. Both were fetched and counted here, and both carry 13 legacy occurrences and no v2.0 terms." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "method observation (tooling defect)" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Found while drafting this thread's 31 records: the records were built with the pipeline's own builders, which add the declaration automatically. Checked 2026-10-31 against the published templates and the ontology source at commit 6ba5f9a97a4511c909f5dfd755c7993dd68989f3. In this repository the declaration appears in swarm (9 files), lean (6), claim-tests (16) and math-bio-theorems (42, being 21 unsigned and 21 signed-test); the hub thread reports that 21 of those were promoted to the production registry on 2026-10-30, which this thread did not verify. The organism thread's own records carry no declaration." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasEvidence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/delphi-v2-template-counts> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Run nanopub-skill's check-nanopub-conformance.py on any record that declares one of these templates. A conforming record reports no unmatched assertion triples and no missing required statement; every v2.0 record checked so far reports both. Republishing the migrated source template and re-checking would settle whether republication is sufficient." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Remediation options, for whoever owns the key and the registry to choose between, not a recommendation from this thread: (1) drop the declaration and state in the publication info why it is absent, which is what this thread's 31 records do; (2) republish the already-migrated source templates, at minimum the claim, evidence-item and rubric-assessment ones, recording the supersedes link, then re-emit records declaring them; (3) migrate the pubinfo-domain and rubric source templates, which are still legacy, before republishing those two; (4) for records already signed or promoted to production, supersede them with corrected copies at the key owner's convenience; (5) leave published records as they are and record the defect against them. Note that a record minting a readable claim identifier would still not conform to the migrated claim template, which fixes the subject to the artifact-code IRI; that is a separate choice each generator makes." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Does not establish: that any published record's assertions are wrong, or that the production records must be retracted. The counts are of namespace occurrences in the files as served and as committed on 2026-10-31; they are not a judgment of whether a template is otherwise correct. How many published records carry the defect was not measured: one was fetched and confirmed, and the rest of that batch is inferred from a local manifest, not from the network." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/delphi-v2-template-declarations-are-false> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/main/organism-design/nanopubs/DECISIONS.md> .
}
